// Both sides generic, so the grant is between corresponding instances — and `Tree` takes one type
// parameter where `Node` takes two, so no instance of `Tree` could ever correspond to one of `Node`.
// Refused where the grant is WRITTEN, not at some call site it silently fails to permit: a grant that
// reads correct and grants nothing is the defect this rule exists to end.
//
// ⚠️ The spelling that says which arguments to use, `friend Tree<K>[peek];`, DOES exist as of KR-80 — and
// this comment used to claim it "does not parse", which was wrong even when it was written: the accessor
// has always been a `qualified_identifier`, which fills `genericArgs`; nothing downstream read them. What
// is refused below is the BARE name, which means the corresponding instance, and no instance of a 1-arg
// `Tree` can correspond to a 2-arg `Node`. Writing the arguments is the way to say something else.
type value Node<K, V> {
    K k; V v; int32 secret;
    friend Tree[peek];               // ERROR: `Tree` takes 1 where `Node` takes 2
    public ctor make(K k, V v) { this.k = k; this.v = v; this.secret = 5; }
    const fn int32 peek() { return this.secret; }
}

type value Tree<K> {
    public int32 n;
    public ctor make() { this.n = 0; }
}

fn int32 main() {
    Tree<int32> t = Tree::<int32>.make();
    return t.n;
}
